+CPU-based, non-differentiable. Choose for debugging or no-GPU environments.
 
 ## Operating characteristics
-*   **Time Complexity:** Sorting is $O(N \log N)$ on the CPU. Rendering is $O(N)$ for the vertex shader and $O(P)$ for the fragment shader, where $P$ is the number of pixels covered by splats.
-*   **Memory Complexity:** $O(N)$ for storing splat data and sorted indices. Textures require ~28 bytes/splat for SH0, ~14 MB VRAM for 500k splats [S5, unofficial].
-*   **Performance:** CPU radix sort of ~500k depth keys takes a few milliseconds on a worker thread [S5, unofficial]. Re-sorting is gated on camera movement to avoid per-frame overhead [S5, unofficial].
-*   **Strengths:** Simple implementation, works on hardware without compute shaders (e.g., older mobile GPUs, WebGL2), leverages standard alpha blending.
-*   **Limitations:** Less efficient than GPU-sorted tile-based methods for very large scenes ($>1$ million splats) due to CPU sorting overhead and lack of early ray termination [S1, S5, unofficial].
-*   **When to Use:** Mobile/web rendering, scenes with moderate splat counts, or when GPU compute capabilities are limited.
-*   **When Not to Use:** Real-time rendering of massive scenes ($>1$M splats) where GPU sorting and tile-based rasterization are required for performance.
+*   **Time Complexity:** Sorting is $O(N \log N)$ on the CPU [S5, unofficial]. Rendering is $O(N \cdot A)$ where $A$ is average splat area in pixels, but offloaded to GPU rasterizer [S1, unofficial].
+*   **Memory Complexity:** $O(N)$ for storing splat data in textures/VBOs [S2, S5, unofficial]. SH0 splats require ~28 bytes/splat in RGBA16F textures [S5, unofficial].
+*   **Measured Performance:** CPU radix sort of ~500k depth keys takes a few milliseconds on a worker thread [S5, unofficial]. Mobile-GS reports up to 127 FPS on modern mobile GPUs using this instanced approach with OIT [S5, unofficial].
+*   **Strengths:** Simple implementation, works on standard graphics APIs (OpenGL/Vulkan/WebGPU) without compute shaders [S5, unofficial], suitable for mobile and web [S5, S6, unofficial].
+*   **Limitations:** Less efficient than tile-based GPU sorting for very large scenes [S1, unofficial], sorting latency on CPU can cause frame drops if not throttled [S5, S6, unofficial].
+*   **When to Use:** Mid-range mobile devices, web browsers, or environments lacking compute shader support [S5, unofficial].
+*   **When Not to Use:** High-end desktop rendering where tile-based GPU rasterization offers better performance [S1, unofficial].
 
 ## Background
-Originating from the paper "3D Gaussian Splatting for Real-Time Radiance Field Rendering" by Kerbl et al. (2023) [S1, unofficial], this rendering implementation was developed to provide a display-only renderer for trained 3DGS scenes. It was notably implemented in the Filament engine (Android/Web) and RViz (ROS) to enable visualization of Gaussian Splatting clouds on client-side hardware [S2, S5, unofficial]. The method addresses the limitation of the original CUDA-based rasterizer by providing a CPU-sorted, instanced approach suitable for broader graphics APIs [S1, S5, unofficial].
+Originated from the paper "3D Gaussian Splatting for Real-Time Radiance Field Rendering" by Kerbl et al. (2023) [S1, unofficial]. This specific implementation variant was developed to enable Gaussian Splatting rendering on platforms without advanced GPU compute capabilities, such as mobile devices and web browsers, using standard instanced drawing techniques [S5, S6, unofficial]. It is used in Android SplatNode, Filament-based renderers, and web viewers [S5, S6, unofficial].
 
